### =====================================================================
### api-gateway - end-to-end requests through the single entry point
###
### Needs: infrastructure (scripts\start-infra.ps1), auth-service (8101) and api-gateway (8080).
###
### Written for the VS Code "REST Client" extension: run requests 1-4 first.
### They log in and the tokens are reused below ({{customerToken}} ...).
### (IntelliJ: copy the accessToken from a login response into the variables by hand.)
###
### Seed users - password for all: Password@123  (see docs/seed-data.md)
### =====================================================================

@gatewayUrl = http://localhost:8080

@customerToken = {{customerLogin.response.body.$.data.accessToken}}
@vendorToken = {{vendorLogin.response.body.$.data.accessToken}}
@adminToken = {{adminLogin.response.body.$.data.accessToken}}
@superAdminToken = {{superAdminLogin.response.body.$.data.accessToken}}


### 1. Log in as a customer (public endpoint, no token needed)
# @name customerLogin
POST {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "customer1@grocery.com",
  "password": "Password@123"
}


### 2. Log in as a vendor (the token contains storeId = 1)
# @name vendorLogin
POST {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "vendor1@grocery.com",
  "password": "Password@123"
}


### 3. Log in as an admin
# @name adminLogin
POST {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@grocery.com",
  "password": "Password@123"
}


### 4. Log in as the super admin
# @name superAdminLogin
POST {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "superadmin@grocery.com",
  "password": "Password@123"
}


### 5. Customer: my account. The gateway turns the token into X-User-Id / X-User-Role for auth-service.
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}


### 6. Vendor: my account (storeId 1 comes from the token)
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{vendorToken}}


### 7. No token -> 401 from the gateway (the request never reaches auth-service)
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me


### 8. Broken token -> 401
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer this.is.not-a-valid-token


### 9. Customer calls an admin endpoint -> 403
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/admin/users
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}


### 10. Admin searches users -> 200
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/admin/users?role=VENDOR&size=5
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}


### 11. Admin calls a super-admin endpoint -> 403
PATCH {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/super-admin/users/7/role
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "role": "ADMIN"
}


### 12. Super admin creates an admin -> 201 (run once; a second run gives 409)
POST {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/super-admin/admins
Authorization: Bearer {{superAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Support Admin",
  "email": "support.admin@grocery.com",
  "phone": "9123400001",
  "password": "Password@123"
}


### 13. Pretending to be the super admin does not work: the gateway replaces X-User-* with the token's values
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
X-User-Id: 1
X-User-Role: SUPER_ADMIN


### 14. A path no service handles yet -> 404 in the standard shape
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/no-such-endpoint
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}


### 15. Internal service-to-service paths are never reachable through the gateway -> 403
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/internal/users/1
Authorization: Bearer {{superAdminToken}}


### 16. Your own correlation id comes back in the response header and appears in the service logs
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
X-Correlation-Id: my-debug-request-001


### 17. Gateway health (includes Redis)
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/actuator/health


### 18. All configured routes
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/actuator/gateway/routes
